      "title": "Center finite sums before taking a limit",
      "html": "<figure class=\"batch4\" id=\"visual-E27\" data-batch4=\"E27\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>E27</span><h3>Center finite sums before taking a limit</h3></header><p>Smaller jumps, the same mean 1 in each layer. Keep N layers and center finitely; their variances have a geometric tail.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-cutoff=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">N=2</button><button type=\"button\" data-cutoff=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"true\">N=4</button><button type=\"button\" data-cutoff=\"6\" aria-pressed=\"false\">N=6</button></div><div class=\"b4-pair\"><section><h4>Compensation inside a finite cutoff</h4><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-means.svg\" alt=\"Unit mean split into ever smaller jumps, subtracted only within a finite sum\" data-means></section><section><h4>Centered variance is summable</h4><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-energy.svg\" alt=\"Unit variance rectangle with exact geometric tail\" data-energy></section></div><div class=\"b4-mobile\"><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-compact.svg\" alt=\"Mobile: finite mean subtraction and geometric variance compared together\" data-compact></div><details class=\"b4-mobile-details\"><summary>Inspect the individual layers</summary><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-means.svg\" alt=\"Full mean structure\" data-detail-means><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-energy.svg\" alt=\"Full variance structure\" data-detail-energy></details><div class=\"b4-warning\">The subdivisions represent <strong>mean count × jump size</strong>, not sampled counts. Centering does not make the fluctuations zero and never starts with “∞−∞”.</div><h4>From one time to a whole path</h4><p class=\"b4-model\">D(t)=Mᴷ(t)−Mᴺ(t), K&gt;N both finite.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-scope=\"end\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Focus on endpoint</button><button type=\"button\" data-scope=\"whole\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Focus on whole interval</button></div><div class=\"b4-single\"><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27-en-4-whole.svg\" alt=\"The endpoint marker and the interval-wide band are different observations\" data-scope-scene></div><details><summary>Second layer: exact model and convergence claims</summary><p>Nk are independent Poisson processes of rates 2^k, Jk(t)=2^(-k)Nk(t), and M^N(t)=Σk≤N[Jk(t)−t], with T=1. First take finite K>N and D=M^K−M^N: the terminal variance is exactly 2^(-N)−2^(-K)≤2^(-N). The martingale maximal inequality gives E sup0≤t≤1|D(t)|²≤4·2^(-N). These finite comparisons precede the limit construction; Fatou then transfers the bound. An unconstructed infinite path is not assumed to prove its own existence.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-E27-end\">The dyadic clocks are those in the text: k≥1 and T=1. Each layer has mean 1 and centered variance 2^(-k); subdivisions show means, not a simulation. M^N=Σk≤N Jk−N subtracts two finite quantities. Divergence of the raw positive-jump sum uses the text’s Chebyshev/Borel–Cantelli argument, not infinite expectation alone. The square tail is vN=Σk>N2^(-k)=2^(-N). For finite K>N, compare D=M^K−M^N: its terminal second moment is exactly 2^(-N)−2^(-K)≤vN. E sup0≤t≤1|D(t)|²≤4vN is a martingale maximal bound, not an equality or deterministic path bound. Fatou transfers the estimate only after constructing the limit. Markov yields ucp from this bound; the general locally uniform a.s. construction also needs the text’s summable-probability/subsequence argument. Filled area is included variance; the unfilled tail retains its true area, without enlargement.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E27.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/E27.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "The dyadic clocks are those in the text: k≥1 and T=1. Each layer has mean 1 and centered variance 2^(-k); subdivisions show means, not a simulation. M^N=Σk≤N Jk−N subtracts two finite quantities. Divergence of the raw positive-jump sum uses the text’s Chebyshev/Borel–Cantelli argument, not infinite expectation alone. The square tail is vN=Σk>N2^(-k)=2^(-N). For finite K>N, compare D=M^K−M^N: its terminal second moment is exactly 2^(-N)−2^(-K)≤vN. E sup0≤t≤1|D(t)|²≤4vN is a martingale maximal bound, not an equality or deterministic path bound. Fatou transfers the estimate only after constructing the limit. Markov yields ucp from this bound; the general locally uniform a.s. construction also needs the text’s summable-probability/subsequence argument. Filled area is included variance; the unfilled tail retains its true area, without enlargement.",
