      "title": "Changing weights does not erase the cost",
      "html": "<figure class=\"batch4\" id=\"visual-E29\" data-batch4=\"E29\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>E29</span><h3>Changing weights does not erase the cost</h3></header><p>For a fixed n the event records stay the same; their weights change. Tilting makes them easier to see, but restoring the old weights retains an exponential cost.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-sample-n=\"64\" aria-pressed=\"false\">n=64</button><button type=\"button\" data-sample-n=\"512\" aria-pressed=\"true\">n=512</button><button type=\"button\" data-sample-n=\"4096\" aria-pressed=\"false\">n=4096</button></div><p class=\"b4-model\" data-event-definition>P: fair coin. Q: head probability 3/4. A512: 352≤K≤416, δ512=1/16.</p><div class=\"b4-pair\"><section><h4>One event, two probabilities</h4><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E29-en-512-event.svg\" alt=\"A common frequency window and true event probability areas\" data-event></section><section><h4>Return weights depend on the record</h4><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E29-en-512-target-record.svg\" alt=\"Head/tail proportions and their product likelihood ratio\" data-record></section></div><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-record-type=\"low\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Lower-edge record</button><button type=\"button\" data-record-type=\"target\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Target-frequency record</button><button type=\"button\" data-record-type=\"high\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Upper-edge record</button></div><div class=\"b4-warning\">Lₙ is a <strong>single-record likelihood ratio</strong>. At finite n, Pn(An) generally differs from Qn(An)e^(−nD).</div><div class=\"b4-single\"><img class=\"b4-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E29-en-512-target-rate.svg\" alt=\"A shrinking interval of possible log weights, with the selected record marked\" data-rate></div><output data-finite-rate>Finite event rate −log Pn(An)/n ≈ 0.07728; limiting rate D ≈ 0.13081.</output><details><summary>Second layer: finite sums, the window and the limit</summary><p>Under P the Xi are iid Bernoulli(1/2); under Q they are iid Bernoulli(3/4), with K=ΣXi. Set δn=1/(2n^(1/3)) and An={|K/n−3/4|≤δn}. Changing n changes both the window and the sample space. Displayed probabilities are rounded finite binomial sums, including combinatorial coefficients, not Gaussian approximations. H^K T^(n−K) represents one ordered record; all records with that K have the same likelihood ratio, not the same combined type probability.</p><p>Here θ=log3, D=(3/4)log3−log2 and −logLn/n=(K/n)log3−log2. On An this differs from D by at most θδn. Chebyshev gives Qn(An complement)≤3/(4n^(1/3))→0, yielding −logPn(An)/n→D. General laws need not realize every target mean by tilting; this is a fully computable interior Bernoulli example.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-E29-end\">Fixed p=1/2, q=3/4; displayed n are 64, 512, 4096. With δn=1/(2n^(1/3)), An={|K/n−q|≤δn}; P and Q use the same event at the same n. Finite binomial sums give exact probabilities, rounded for display. Subpixel positive masses are not enlarged or treated as zero. The record likelihood ratio Ln=2^n/3^K varies with K and can exceed one for some finite parameters; a whole event cannot be reweighted by one constant. For θ=log3 and D=qlog3−log2, exp[−n(D+θδn)]Qn(An)≤Pn(An)≤exp[−n(D−θδn)]Qn(An). Only δn→0 and Qn(An)→1 give limiting rate D at speed n. A single frequency cell does not have Q-probability tending to one. This does not restrict the general Cramér theorem to tilt-matched targets.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v4/E29.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/E29.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Fixed p=1/2, q=3/4; displayed n are 64, 512, 4096. With δn=1/(2n^(1/3)), An={|K/n−q|≤δn}; P and Q use the same event at the same n. Finite binomial sums give exact probabilities, rounded for display. Subpixel positive masses are not enlarged or treated as zero. The record likelihood ratio Ln=2^n/3^K varies with K and can exceed one for some finite parameters; a whole event cannot be reweighted by one constant. For θ=log3 and D=qlog3−log2, exp[−n(D+θδn)]Qn(An)≤Pn(An)≤exp[−n(D−θδn)]Qn(An). Only δn→0 and Qn(An)→1 give limiting rate D at speed n. A single frequency cell does not have Q-probability tending to one. This does not restrict the general Cramér theorem to tilt-matched targets.",
